WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.760
A count comes out low.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.480
Were the missing neurons never made, or made and then lost?

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:15.380
In 1934, Viktor Hamburger removed one wing bud from chick embryos about three days

4
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:16.380
old.

5
00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:21.803
Days later, the operated side had fewer motor neurons than the intact side, its

6
00:00:21.803 --> 00:00:22.803
control.

7
00:00:23.267 --> 00:00:27.307
Hamburger read this as the limb setting how many neurons the cord makes.

8
00:00:28.317 --> 00:00:29.857
That reading fits the count.

9
00:00:30.817 --> 00:00:35.077
So do others. Think of a bakery whose shelf ends the day half empty.

10
00:00:36.117 --> 00:00:39.477
It might have baked fewer, sent some away, or baked some as rolls.

11
00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.830
Or it baked a full batch and threw half away.

12
00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:45.390
The shelf looks the same every time.

13
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.670
A low neuron count is that shelf.

14
00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.750
A count made only at the end cannot tell the histories apart.

15
00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:56.990
Pause, and predict.

16
00:00:57.910 --> 00:01:01.310
What would you count, and when, to tell never made from made and then lost?

17
00:01:07.783 --> 00:01:10.623
Count at closely spaced stages, on both sides.

18
00:01:11.543 --> 00:01:13.723
Count healthy neurons, and count dying ones.

19
00:01:14.733 --> 00:01:18.333
A dying neuron often has a small, dark, condensed nucleus.

20
00:01:19.193 --> 00:01:20.333
It is called pyknotic.

21
00:01:21.316 --> 00:01:26.456
Never made: the operated side never catches up, and no extra dying cells appear.

22
00:01:27.499 --> 00:01:29.219
But dying cells are cleared fast.

23
00:01:30.139 --> 00:01:33.839
So count often, and birthdate the cells, before trusting a missing hump.

24
00:01:34.783 --> 00:01:37.243
Made, then lost: the sides start equal.

25
00:01:38.123 --> 00:01:40.743
Then dying cells appear, and the count falls behind.

26
00:01:44.216 --> 00:01:49.796
In wartime Italy, Rita Levi-Montalcini and Giuseppe Levi studied staged sections

27
00:01:49.796 --> 00:01:52.176
like these, in a laboratory at home.

28
00:01:53.149 --> 00:01:56.989
They found neurons that had already formed, and were now degenerating.

29
00:01:57.769 --> 00:01:59.149
The missing neurons had been made.

30
00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:05.813
In 1949, her joint paper with Hamburger, on the sensory neurons of chick spinal

31
00:02:05.813 --> 00:02:07.673
ganglia, supported that reading.

32
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:12.443
The target's main job was not to call neurons into existence.

33
00:02:13.403 --> 00:02:14.523
It was to keep them alive.

34
00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:21.673
In 1975, Hamburger counted the death itself, in normal embryos with all their limbs.

35
00:02:22.766 --> 00:02:25.286
The leg's motor neurons peaked near day 6.

36
00:02:26.166 --> 00:02:29.426
Then about two in five of them died, most within three days.

37
00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:39.109
In 1976, Margaret Hollyday and Hamburger grafted an extra leg beside the normal one.

38
00:02:40.083 --> 00:02:45.383
Predict. If more target meant more births, where would the grafted side's count be

39
00:02:45.383 --> 00:02:46.383
at day 6?

40
00:02:52.383 --> 00:02:57.683
At day 6, before the normal loss, the grafted side had no more motor neurons than

41
00:02:57.683 --> 00:02:58.683
the control.

42
00:02:59.233 --> 00:03:03.073
Later, in embryos whose grafts moved vigorously, it had more.

43
00:03:03.973 --> 00:03:05.553
About a tenth to over a quarter more.

44
00:03:09.416 --> 00:03:11.516
More target did not mean more births.

45
00:03:12.356 --> 00:03:13.356
It meant fewer deaths.

46
00:03:14.266 --> 00:03:18.766
Takeaway: a count tells you a history only if you also counted along the way.
